[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"NIO (New I/O): Channels, Buffers, and Selectors"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"NIO, introduced in Java 1.4, provides a different approach to I/O operations compared to the traditional `java.io` package. NIO focuses on non-blocking I/O, which allows a single thread to manage multiple channels simultaneously, improving performance and scalability, especially in network programming."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Channels"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Channels represent connections to I/O services, such as files and sockets. They are similar to streams but are bidirectional, allowing both reading and writing. Key Channel implementations include:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`FileChannel`: For reading and writing files."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`SocketChannel`: For TCP network communication."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`ServerSocketChannel`: Listens for incoming TCP connections."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`DatagramChannel`: For UDP network communication."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike streams, channels cannot be directly created. You obtain them from an existing I/O object, such as a `FileInputStream`, `FileOutputStream`, or a `Socket`."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.RandomAccessFile;
import java.nio.channels.FileChannel;

public class FileChannelExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (RandomAccessFile file = new RandomAccessFile("data.txt", "rw");
             FileChannel channel = file.getChannel()) {

            // Perform operations with the channel
            System.out.println("File channel opened.");

        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Buffers"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Buffers are containers for data. They are used to read data from and write data to Channels. NIO provides several buffer types, each for a specific data type:"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`ByteBuffer`: For storing bytes."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`CharBuffer`: For storing characters."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`ShortBuffer`: For storing short integers."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`IntBuffer`: For storing integers."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`LongBuffer`: For storing long integers."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`FloatBuffer`: For storing floating-point numbers."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`DoubleBuffer`: For storing double-precision floating-point numbers."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Buffers have three key properties:"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Capacity`: The maximum number of elements the buffer can hold."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Position`: The index of the next element to be read or written."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`Limit`: The index of the first element that should not be read or written. Effectively, it defines how much of the buffer is used."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common buffer operations include:"},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`allocate()`: Creates a new buffer with a specified capacity."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`put()`: Writes data into the buffer."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`get()`: Reads data from the buffer."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`flip()`: Prepares the buffer for reading by setting the limit to the current position and resetting the position to 0."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`clear()`: Prepares the buffer for writing by resetting the position to 0 and setting the limit to the capacity."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`rewind()`: Resets the position to 0, allowing data to be re-read."},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

public class BufferExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Allocate a ByteBuffer with a capacity of 1024 bytes
        ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);

        // Write data to the buffer
        String message = "Hello, NIO!";
        buffer.put(message.getBytes());

        // Flip the buffer to prepare for reading
        buffer.flip();

        // Read data from the buffer
        byte[] bytes = new byte[buffer.limit()];
        buffer.get(bytes);

        String receivedMessage = new String(bytes);
        System.out.println("Received message: " + receivedMessage);

        // Clear the buffer for future use
        buffer.clear();
    }
}"},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Selectors"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Selectors enable a single thread to monitor multiple channels for events, such as readability and writability. This is the core of non-blocking I/O. A Selector allows you to register multiple channels and then select the channels that are ready for I/O operations."},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Key steps in using Selectors:"},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create a Selector using `Selector.open()`."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Register channels with the Selector using `channel.register(selector, interestOps)`. `interestOps` specifies the events you are interested in (e.g., `SelectionKey.OP_READ`, `SelectionKey.OP_WRITE`, `SelectionKey.OP_CONNECT`, `SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT`)."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Call `selector.select()` to block until at least one channel is ready for an event, or until a timeout expires."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Get the selected keys using `selector.selectedKeys()`. This returns a set of `SelectionKey` objects, each representing a channel that is ready for an event."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Iterate through the selected keys and perform the appropriate I/O operation on the corresponding channel."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Remove the processed key from the selected keys set to avoid processing it again."},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.InetSocketAddress;
import java.nio.ByteBuffer;
import java.nio.channels.SelectionKey;
import java.nio.channels.Selector;
import java.nio.channels.ServerSocketChannel;
import java.nio.channels.SocketChannel;
import java.util.Iterator;
import java.util.Set;

public class SelectorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        // Create a Selector
        Selector selector = Selector.open();

        // Create a ServerSocketChannel and configure it as non-blocking
        ServerSocketChannel serverSocketChannel = ServerSocketChannel.open();
        serverSocketChannel.socket().bind(new InetSocketAddress("localhost", 8080));
        serverSocketChannel.configureBlocking(false);

        // Register the ServerSocketChannel with the Selector for accept events
        serverSocketChannel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);

        System.out.println("Server started on port 8080");

        while (true) {
            // Block until at least one channel is ready for an event
            int readyChannels = selector.select();

            if (readyChannels == 0) continue;

            // Get the selected keys
            Set<SelectionKey> selectedKeys = selector.selectedKeys();
            Iterator<SelectionKey> keyIterator = selectedKeys.iterator();

            while (keyIterator.hasNext()) {
                SelectionKey key = keyIterator.next();

                if (key.isAcceptable()) {
                    // Accept a new connection
                    ServerSocketChannel serverChannel = (ServerSocketChannel) key.channel();
                    SocketChannel clientChannel = serverChannel.accept();
                    clientChannel.configureBlocking(false);

                    // Register the client channel with the Selector for read events
                    clientChannel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_READ);

                    System.out.println("Accepted new connection from: " + clientChannel.getRemoteAddress());
                } else if (key.isReadable()) {
                    // Read data from a client channel
                    SocketChannel channel = (SocketChannel) key.channel();
                    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocate(1024);
                    int bytesRead = channel.read(buffer);

                    if (bytesRead > 0) {
                        buffer.flip();
                        byte[] bytes = new byte[buffer.limit()];
                        buffer.get(bytes);
                        String message = new String(bytes);
                        System.out.println("Received message from " + channel.getRemoteAddress() + ": " + message);
                    } else if (bytesRead == -1) {
                        // Client disconnected
                        channel.close();
                        key.cancel();
                        System.out.println("Connection closed by: " + channel.getRemoteAddress());
                    }
                }

                // Remove the key from the selected keys set
                keyIterator.remove();
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"This example demonstrates a simple server that uses a Selector to accept connections and read data from multiple clients.  It's a basic illustration of the power of NIO for handling concurrent I/O operations."},{"order":43,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"NIO provides a more efficient and scalable approach to I/O compared to the traditional `java.io` package. By using Channels, Buffers, and Selectors, you can build high-performance applications that handle multiple concurrent connections with a single thread. Understanding these concepts is crucial for developing robust and scalable network applications in Java."}]
